Great — I now have a solid map of the system, and the news is good: frequency-domain work fits Stack's architecture almost suspiciously well. Let me lay out the design space, then flag the real decision points.

Why this fits your three-stream model so well
Here's the core insight that shapes everything else:

A mask drawn in the frequency domain is a filter. That's literally what frequency-domain filtering is — multiply the spectrum by a 2D function. Your existing single-channel mask stream is, by definition, a 2D function over the image plane. If that plane is reinterpreted as frequency space, every mask tool you already have becomes a filter-design tool.

So the three streams map onto frequency work cleanly:

Stream	Spatial meaning	Frequency meaning
Image (RGBA16F)	color pixels	complex spectrum (needs a packing decision — see below)
Mask (single-channel)	region/spatial weight	transfer function / filter shape
Math	pixel arithmetic	spectrum arithmetic, filter synthesis
This means a huge amount of frequency processing can be built without new stream types — by reusing your mask infrastructure to draw filters. That's the elegant payoff.

Node taxonomy (respecting "one node, one job")
Following the DataMath template (one NodeKind + Mode enum + one shader file), here's a clean decomposition:

Core transform pair

FFT (forward) — spatial image → complex spectrum. One job: transform.
IFFT (inverse) — complex spectrum → spatial image. One job: inverse transform.
Visualization (you specifically asked about this)

SpectrumView — complex spectrum → viewable image. Applies fftshift (DC to center), log(1+|F|) dynamic-range compression, and a false-color LUT. This is the node that makes the invisible visible. Output is a normal Image stream so it can be viewed/saved/blended.
Filter-shape generators (output Masks, in frequency space)

FrequencyMask — one node, Mode enum: LowPass / HighPass / BandPass / BandStop / Notch / Gaussian / Butterworth, with cutoff/radius/order params. Outputs a single-channel mask representing the filter shape. This is the natural home for "design a filter."
Optional later: NotchMask as its own node when interactive notch placement wants dedicated UI.
Spectrum arithmetic (mirrors DataMath)

SpectrumMath — multiply spectrum by mask (the fundamental filtering op), or by another spectrum; add/subtract spectrums (frequency separation). Same shape as DataMath, complex-aware.
MagnitudePhase — split a spectrum into magnitude (mask) + phase (mask), or recombine. Enables the classic "phase-only reconstruction" demo and independent band editing.
Analysis (uses your existing Analysis socket type)

SpectrumAnalyzer — radial average / band energy / dominant frequencies. Feeds numeric readouts and your analysis stream.
That's ~7 nodes for a complete, composable frequency toolset — each doing one thing, all following existing conventions.

How to display it visually
This matters because Stack's identity is "extremely visually pleasing," and the frequency domain is genuinely gorgeous when rendered well.

Magnitude spectrum (the workhorse view)

fftshift — move DC from corner to center so frequencies increase radially outward
log(1 + |F(u,v)|) — dynamic range of a raw spectrum is ~6 orders of magnitude; log is mandatory or you see one bright dot
False-color LUT — viridis/turbo/inferno look stunning and read well; a custom Stack-branded LUT would be on-brand. Grayscale is the textbook default but wastes the visual identity.
Optional gamma/contrast controls on the view node.
Natural images produce a characteristic bright-cross-along-axes pattern that's immediately recognizable — and periodic noise (moiré, scan lines, sensor fixed-pattern noise) shows up as discrete bright spikes off-center, which a user can then paint out.

Phase spectrum

Less visually intuitive; map [-π, π] to grayscale. Rarely edited directly but useful for diagnostics.
The flagship interaction (later phase)

Paint directly on the SpectrumView to suppress frequencies — each brush stroke becomes a notch filter. Removing periodic noise by clicking bright spikes is the pro technique and feels magical. This is a natural extension once the core nodes exist.
The real architectural decisions
These are genuine forks — your call drives the implementation heavily. I'd want your input before planning any code:

Compute shader vs. fragment-shader ping-pong. Your GL context is already 4.3 core, glDispatchCompute is loaded, and CreateComputeProgram() exists at GLHelpers.cpp:112 — but is called nowhere. An FFT is the textbook first citizen for compute (Cooley-Tukey butterfly with shared memory). The alternative is multi-pass fragment shaders into ping-pong textures (Stockham auto-sort), which matches how smoothFragSrc and RawDetailFusion already work. Compute is faster and cleaner; fragment ping-pong is lower-risk and consistent with the codebase.
What flows on the wire for a spectrum. Three options, ranked by plumbing cost:
(C) Decompose into two Mask streams — magnitude + phase. Maximally reuses your existing mask infra; filtering becomes ordinary mask math. Can't do true complex arithmetic, but amplitude filtering (the 95% case) works perfectly. My lean for Stack specifically.
(A) New SocketType::Spectrum — cleanest type safety, most plumbing (new pin type, type-checking, ~2 extra switch statements).
(B) Pack complex into RG channels of an Image-stream texture — least new plumbing, but breaks type safety (a spectrum could connect to a regular blur and produce garbage).
Precision / texture format. FFTs accumulate numerical error badly. RGBA16F (your pipeline default) is risky for large transforms; RG32F/RGBA32F is safer but is a new working format for the codebase (CreateStorageTexture(w,h,internalFormat) already supports it). Probably want 32F internally for the transform, down-convert for view/output.
Power-of-two vs. arbitrary sizes. Classic radix-2 FFT needs Po2 dims; real images aren't. Options: zero-pad to next Po2 and crop (simple, slight cost), or Bluestein/chirp-z for arbitrary sizes (more code). For an image editor, padding is usually fine.
Color handling. FFT each RGB channel independently (3× cost, full color frequency info), or luminance-only (faster, loses chroma frequency). Many pro tools do luminance for the heavy work; a per-channel mode is a nice option.
The strategic opportunity
Worth calling out separately: a frequency node is the most natural possible catalyst for turning on compute shaders in Stack. The infrastructure is loaded, context is compatible, the helper is written — it's just waiting for a first user. If you ever want GPU compute for anything else (deconvolution, simulations, big convolutions), enabling it via FFT is the cleanest entry point. That said, it's also a commitment — first compute user writes the conventions everyone else follows.

A pragmatic MVP
If I were scoping a first deliverable, I'd suggest:

FFT + IFFT + SpectrumView + FrequencyMask (LowPass/HighPass/BandPass/Notch) + SpectrumMath (multiply).

That's enough to: see a spectrum, design a filter visually or by parameter, apply it, and reconstruct. It validates the whole pipeline and the precision/stream decisions before you build the fancier nodes (magnitude/phase split, analyzer, interactive painting). Implementation as fragment-shader ping-pong first (matches codebase), with compute as a later optimization once the math and UX are proven.

