Plan
Frequency / Fourier Node System — Implementation Plan
What we're building
A complete, composable frequency-domain toolset for Stack's node graph — 7 new node kinds, each doing one job, all matching existing conventions exactly. This is also the first feature in Stack to use compute shaders (GPU compute), switching on infrastructure that's already loaded but unused.

Your three decisions, locked in:

Connections: freely mixable — frequency data rides the existing Image wires (complex numbers packed into texture channels). Plugs anywhere; raw spectrum fed to a normal node just looks like noise until transformed back.
GPU method: compute shaders (GL 4.3, already loaded: glDispatchCompute, glMemoryBarrier, glBindImageTexture, glBindBufferBase, CreateComputeProgram at GLHelpers.cpp:112).
Spectrum look: all four (Turbo / Viridis / Inferno / Grayscale) via a dropdown on the view node.
Plus my engineering calls: high-precision (RG32F) math internally, arbitrary image sizes (zero-pad + crop), per-node luminance-or-RGB toggle.

The 7 nodes
#	Node	Purpose	Input → Output
1	FFT	Forward transform: spatial image → complex spectrum	Image → Image (complex-packed)
2	IFFT	Inverse transform: spectrum → spatial image	Image → Image
3	Spectrum View	Visualization: spectrum → viewable false-color image (LUT dropdown, log scaling, fftshift)	Image → Image
4	Frequency Mask	Filter-shape generator (LowPass/HighPass/BandPass/BandStop/Notch/Gaussian/Butterworth)	(params) → Mask
5	Spectrum Math	Multiply spectrum by mask (= filtering), or spectrum±spectrum	Image + Mask → Image
6	Magnitude / Phase	Split a spectrum into magnitude (Mask) + phase (Mask), or recombine	Image → Mask, Mask (and inverse)
7	Spectrum Analyzer	Numeric readouts: radial average, band energy, dominant frequencies	Image → Analysis
MVP ordering: nodes 1,2,3,4,5 first (proves the whole pipeline); 6,7 second.

The compute-shader FFT design
Algorithm: Cooley-Tukey radix-2 butterfly. Because real images aren't power-of-two, the FFT node zero-pads to the next power of two (in a padded RG32F texture), transforms, and the IFFT node crops back to the original size.

Two-phase, separable (rows then columns): 2D FFT = FFT every row, then FFT every column. Each phase is log₂(N) butterfly passes. Each pass is one compute dispatch writing into a ping-pong texture pair via image2D load/store + glMemoryBarrier(GL_TEXTURE_FETCH_BARRIER_BIT | GL_SHADER_IMAGE_ACCESS_BARRIER_BIT) between passes.

Complex packing: RG32F texture — .r = real, .g = imaginary. For 3-channel color, pack one channel per texture layer or run the pipeline 3×. Luminance mode (default) runs once on luma.

Why compute, not fragment ping-pong: the butterfly needs scattered reads (Stockham auto-sort avoids in-place permutation but still benefits from shared memory). Compute with image2D is the clean fit. The existing CreateComputeProgram (GLHelpers.cpp:112) and glDispatchCompute loader (GLLoader.cpp:138) are ready.

New shared infrastructure (written once, reused forever):

src/Renderer/Frequency/GpuFft.h/.cpp — the FFT engine class: Forward(), Inverse(), owns butterfly compute programs, padded textures, ping-pong pair. This becomes the template every future compute-shader feature in Stack copies.
