Implementation phases (buildable & testable at each step)
Phase A — Compute foundation (no UI yet): Build GpuFft engine + a hidden validation path. Verify with a known test image (impulse → constant spectrum; constant → DC spike). .\build.cmd passes. Exit criterion: round-trip FFT→IFFT reconstructs the input to within numerical tolerance.

Phase B — Core transform nodes (FFT, IFFT): Wire the two node kinds through all ~16 editor+renderer sites. Add to catalog under "Frequency". Nodes appear, transform runs, output flows on the wire. Exit criterion: connect Image → FFT → IFFT → Output, image reconstructs.

Phase C — Spectrum View node: The visualization with the 4-LUT dropdown, log scaling, fftshift. This is the "wow" node — the moment you can see a spectrum. Exit criterion: a real photo shows the characteristic cross + bright DC center, switchable LUTs.

Phase D — Frequency Mask + Spectrum Math (the payoff): Design filters parametrically, multiply into a spectrum, reconstruct. Low-pass = blur, high-pass = sharpen, notch = kill periodic noise. Exit criterion: notch a moiré/photo scan-line artifact and watch it vanish on reconstruction.

Phase E — Magnitude/Phase + Spectrum Analyzer (advanced): Split/recombine, numeric band readouts into the existing Analysis stream. Exit criterion: phase-only reconstruction demo works; analyzer reports sensible band energies.

Phase F — Polish: tooltips, sensible parameter ranges, default values, node-body preview where applicable, and (per your docs rules) any documentation routed through docs/stack-documentation protocols.

Documentation note (per your rules)
Per AGENTS.md, if I touch docs I'll read the entry file first (IDEA_INTAKE_PROTOCOL.md for notes, or the relevant develop/raw protocol) and follow Stack's strict documentation conventions. Documentation is optional here — I'll only create it if you want it, and I'll flag it before writing.

